WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.680
The Many Saints of Newark, the Sopranos prequel&nbsp;
from writer-producer David Chase and director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.960
Alan Taylor, has finally been released. The movie&nbsp;
follows the complicated life of mobster Dickie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.240
Moltisanti, including his relationship with young&nbsp;
Tony Soprano — and so far it has proven a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.840
divisive among long-time fans of the landmark&nbsp;
HBO series. Sadly, this probably isn't the kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.520
of buzz Warner Bros. was hoping for after finally&nbsp;
returning to David Chase's popular mob universe.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.080
The movie is also the latest experimental&nbsp;
offering in the dual-release strategy Warner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.520
Bros. has been deploying throughout&nbsp;
the pandemic. Some of the biggest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.160
most-anticipated releases distributed by&nbsp;
the studio have been given simultaneous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:46.480
day-and-date debuts in theaters and on the HBO Max&nbsp;
streaming service. Thus far, the experiment has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.840
been a mixed bag — at least as far as box office&nbsp;
returns are concerned. Some of these films have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.520
had successful theatrical runs that haven't been&nbsp;
entirely cannibalized by their HBO Max releases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:04.000
including Godzilla vs. Kong and Mortal Kombat. But&nbsp;
not all of Warner's films have been so successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.240
and The Many Saints of Newark has&nbsp;
landed squarely in the latter category.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:09.840
"Oh Marone, oh, Jesus."

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.560
The Many Saints of Newark pulled in a paltry $5&nbsp;
million at the box office in its first weekend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.040
of release, an underperformance even by the&nbsp;
diminished standards of the pandemic era.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.960
Box Office Pro projected before the&nbsp;
weekend that Many Saints would gross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.400
somewhere in the range of $7-12&nbsp;
million. So why has this happened?

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.800
Well, there's no smoking gun for why&nbsp;
the Sopranos prequel didn't kill it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.800
at the box office. But it seems likely&nbsp;
that a combination of factors may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.520
worked together to reduce the&nbsp;
movie's box office takings.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.120
According to a recent analysis&nbsp;
by Deadline, the main reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.520
could be that the target audience for The&nbsp;
Many Saints of Newark is an older demographic;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.040
one that has been slow to return&nbsp;
to theaters over coronavirus fears.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.360
The most successful films of the pandemic&nbsp;
era have been tentpole films that appeal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.120
to younger viewers, such as Black Widow, which&nbsp;
grossed $80.8 million on its opening weekend.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.120
"Oof, Marone."

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.440
Certainly, it would be interesting to see&nbsp;
data on how Many Saints performed on HBO Max.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.560
Enough people may have watched it on the&nbsp;
streaming service for Warner Bros. to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.600
happy with it. And HBO just inked a massive&nbsp;
new overall deal with David Chase, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.840
so they definitely seem bullish on the potential&nbsp;
for future Sopranos content. Nobody can really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.800
say for sure whether the movie was a total&nbsp;
flop until those HBO figures are released.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.560
The other reason why the movie might&nbsp;
not be crushing at the box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.240
could be word-of-mouth. There are a&nbsp;
lot of young Sopranos fans out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.760
but they didn't show up, either. The movie has&nbsp;
a mediocre C+ audience rating from CinemaScore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.000
and has gotten a lukewarm response&nbsp;
from Sopranos fans on social media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.240
If that's the case, then any Sopranos fans&nbsp;
who do want to check out the new movie might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.600
do so on the streaming service they already pay&nbsp;
for, instead of shelling out for movie tickets.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.680
More than anything else, however, there's a&nbsp;
250-pound symbiote-shaped elephant in the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.400
here; a massive rival to The Many Saints of Newark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.600
that undeniably sucked up the box office&nbsp;
oxygen on this balmy October weekend.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.000
"There's gonna be carnage."

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.560
The Many Saints of Newark landed in theaters&nbsp;
and on HBO Max on October 1st — the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:24.720
day the Woody Harrelson and Tom Hardy flick&nbsp;
Venom: Let There Be Carnage got its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.440
amid much fan anticipation. Notably,&nbsp;
Let There Be Carnage did not receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:33.520
a day-and-date streaming release from&nbsp;
Sony Pictures, one of the major studios&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.160
most reluctant to embrace the dual-release&nbsp;
strategy preferred by Warner Bros. and Disney.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.480
In the end, Sony's gamble on a pandemic&nbsp;
weekend at the theater paid off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.720
dethroning Black Widow to become the&nbsp;
highest-grossing theatrical release of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.560
pandemic era. With so many moviegoers flocking to&nbsp;
see Hardy's Venom square off against Harrelson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.760
Carnage, it seems like there just wasn't much&nbsp;
market share left for Many Saints to claim.

